Right now, there are some inlined styles automatically set for the outer `<div>` of an HTML output table:

```
style="overflow-x:auto;overflow-y:auto;width:auto;height:auto;"
```

While these are pretty good, we are missing top and bottom padding, making tables look poorly laid out in R Markdown documents. Proposed values might be `padding-top: 10px;` and `padding-bottom: 15px;`. 

Another option is placing a ruleset for the the `<div>` `id` inside of the `<style>` block as such:

```
#<table_id> {
  overflow-x:auto;
  overflow-y:auto;
  width:auto;
  height:auto;
  padding-top: 10px;
  padding-bottom: 15px;
}
```

With this change, it would be possible to add more rules to the outer div with `opt_css()` and enable whatever customizations are needed.

Currently, `title` and `subtitle` arguments in `gt::tab_header()` are rendered as `<th>` tag for html output.

However, please use `<td>` and just rely on css for their visual aspects.

`<th>` is semantically associated with either column or row headers, so using `<th>` tag for title and subtitle causes significant confusion for assistive technologies (e.g., screen readers or robots).

Now that we surround title and subtitle inside `<thead>`, that suffices.

## Reprex

    # Load package
    library(gt)

    # Create gt table with title and subtitle
    airquality[1:10, ] %>%
      gt::gt() %>%
      gt::tab_header(
        title = "Title",
        subtitle = "Subtitle"
      )

