In a gt table rendered as HTML, the top line of a cell border (created using `tab_style()` -> `cell_borders()`) is not visible unless it is `2px` or larger. The table body row lines currently have a width of `"thin"`, which is `1px`.

Here is a reproducible example with screenshot, where `cell_borders()` without any arguments draws a `1px` box at each location:

```r
library(gt)
library(tidyverse)

exibble %>%
  select(num, date, datetime) %>%
  gt() %>%
  tab_style(
    style = cell_borders(),
    locations = cells_data(
      columns = vars(num),
      rows = num > 100 & num < 10000)
  )
```

<img width="431" alt="top-border-missing" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5612024/63184076-90128800-c024-11e9-81b6-cd5e978390bb.png">

The solution to this shouldn't have anything to do with increased specificity when drawing border lines (these are already done as inline `style`s) but, likely, to change the drawing order of the row lines. Currently row lines are part of the `gt_row` CSS class:

```scss
  .gt_row {
    padding: $row_padding; /* row.padding */
    margin: 10px;
    border-bottom-style: solid;
    border-bottom-width: thin;
    border-bottom-color: #D3D3D3;
    vertical-align: middle;
    overflow-x: hidden;
  }
```

My thought is that we could substitute all `border-bottom-*` rules for `border-top-*` rules and  avoid the line-drawing conflict between row lines and border lines altogether.

The `html()` helper function does not work with `label` in  `tab_spanner()`. 

---

``` r
library(tidyverse)
library(gt)

airquality_m <- 
  airquality %>%
  mutate(Year = 1973L) %>%
  slice(1:10) %>%
  select(Month, Day, Year)
  
# Create a display table using the `airquality`
# dataset; arrange columns into groups
gt_tbl <- 
  gt(data = airquality_m) %>%
  tab_header(
    title = "New York Air Quality Measurements",
    subtitle = "Daily measurements in New York City (May 1-10, 1973)"
  ) %>%
  tab_spanner(
    label = html("Time <br> n = 10"),
    columns = vars(Year, Month, Day)
  )

# Show the gt Table
gt_tbl
```

